<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Saadian Tombs</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">16th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Saadian Tombs are the royal necropolis of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saadi-dynasty">Saadi dynasty</a> beside the Kasbah Mosque in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marrakesh">Marrakesh</a>. Built mainly under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-al-mansur">Ahmad al-Mansur</a> in the late sixteenth century, they include the richly decorated Hall of the Twelve Columns with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carrara">Carrara</a> marble and carved cedar. The Alaouite sultan Mulay Ismail walled off the site, and it was rediscovered by the French in 1917.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/saadian-tombs.jpg" alt="Saadian Tombs" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: C messier · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Saadian_Tombs_1044-HDR.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
